<edge source="ai_global_rivalry::model_release_cadence" target="china_regional_power::foreign_scrutiny_intensi" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">A quicker cadence of publicly available AI model releases increases observable capability proliferation and novel use-cases, triggering faster regulatory inquiries and intelligence attention from external governments toward the originators.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">ai_global_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">china_regional_power</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ai_global_rivalry::model_release_cadence" target="russia_europe_war::hybrid_attack_frequency" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">A faster cadence of released AI models rapidly expands widely accessible automation, generative code, planning and targeting tools that state and proxy actors can repurpose for cyber intrusions, automated disinformation campaigns and drone/autonomous targeting, raising the rate of hybrid attacks in the Russia–Europe theater.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">ai_global_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_europe_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ai_global_rivalry::model_release_cadence" target="us_iran_standoff::export_route_security" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Faster release cadence of capable models lowers the technical barrier for building autonomous navigation/targeting software that non-state maritime actors and proxies adapt to harass and strike commercial shipping, increasing interdictions and degrading export-route security.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">ai_global_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_standoff</data>
</edge>
