<edge source="bond_market_shock::investor_liquidity_flows" target="ai_global_rivalry::investment_flow_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Net inflows into fixed‑income and liquid vehicles free up allocable capital for venture and growth funds and reduce dry powder drawdowns, so a drop in investor liquidity directly forces managers to cut new AI investments and an increase directly enables more deal‑making.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">bond_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_global_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bond_market_shock::investor_liquidity_flows" target="china_regional_power::fiscal_subsidy_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">Sudden net outflows from fixed‑income and credit funds tighten domestic credit availability and pressure employment in exposed sectors, prompting authorities to deploy or expand targeted fiscal subsidies to stabilize firms, jobs and domestic demand.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">bond_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">china_regional_power</data>
</edge>
<edge source="bond_market_shock::investor_liquidity_flows" target="russia_europe_war::market_expectations_of_s" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Net inflows into fixed-income and related funds restore trade-finance and commodity-backed lending capacity for exporters and traders, directly reducing market expectations of imminent supply disruptions; conversely outflows raise those expectations.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">bond_market_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_europe_war</data>
</edge>
